1
00:01:40,233 --> 00:01:42,202
I got it!

2
00:01:42,936 --> 00:01:45,098
Nobody got it!

3
00:01:46,607 --> 00:01:48,473
Look out, man.

4
00:01:59,019 --> 00:02:01,818
Hi. Sorry I'm late. There was
a big accident on the parkway.

5
00:02:01,989 --> 00:02:03,629
I got stuck behind a
bunch of lookie loos.

6
00:02:03,757 --> 00:02:07,057
That's all right. We should get started,
Ben called about 15 minutes ago,

7
00:02:07,227 --> 00:02:08,661
said he was leaving
the courthouse,

8
00:02:08,829 --> 00:02:10,549
he was gonna stop and
pick up some barbecue,

9
00:02:10,697 --> 00:02:13,189
so that only gives
us 30 minutes.

10
00:02:13,667 --> 00:02:14,999
Pop, what are you doing?

11
00:02:15,469 --> 00:02:17,597
Looking for Ben's
popcorn popper.

12
00:02:17,771 --> 00:02:19,535
Forget the popcorn popper.

13
00:02:19,706 --> 00:02:21,504
How am I gonna pop the popcorn?

14
00:02:21,675 --> 00:02:23,268
Look, come on, we
gotta get going here.

15
00:02:23,443 --> 00:02:26,106
Go ahead. Ah, here it is.

16
00:02:26,279 --> 00:02:27,713
Now, where's the popcorn?

17
00:02:28,915 --> 00:02:31,885
Thursday the 27th, 5 p.m.
Is that okay with everyone?

18
00:02:32,052 --> 00:02:33,315
- Yeah, good.
- Yeah, yeah.

19
00:02:33,487 --> 00:02:35,820
Good, because I spoke
to Sandy. Guess what.

20
00:02:35,989 --> 00:02:38,584
- She said it's okay?
- As long as we're out of the building

21
00:02:38,759 --> 00:02:40,399
- by midnight, it doesn't matter.
- Great.

22
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:42,620
- Yeah, I thought so too.
- That's great.

23
00:02:42,796 --> 00:02:45,197
- Food?
- I'll have it catered.

24
00:02:45,365 --> 00:02:47,960
Caviar, designer
cake, stuff like that.

25
00:02:48,135 --> 00:02:50,127
No, Billy, hot dogs
and french fries.

26
00:02:50,303 --> 00:02:51,532
Okay.

27
00:02:51,705 --> 00:02:54,641
And popcorn, if I
can ever find any.

28
00:02:56,510 --> 00:02:57,773
This reminds me of something.

29
00:02:57,944 --> 00:03:01,608
Uh, Pop, this is a surprise
party. We're all clear on that, right?

30
00:03:02,649 --> 00:03:04,447
Tick a lock.

31
00:03:07,721 --> 00:03:09,053
What are you looking at me for?

32
00:03:09,489 --> 00:03:11,822
Well, remember when
you took Ben to the movies

33
00:03:11,992 --> 00:03:13,392
- last month?
- Yeah.

34
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:16,587
Walked out after the first 10 minutes
because you blurted out the ending.

35
00:03:17,364 --> 00:03:20,232
Okay, one lousy
little slip of the tongue,

36
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:22,062
and you're marked for life.

37
00:03:22,235 --> 00:03:25,831
And I was right. It did
end that way. Heh-heh-heh.

38
00:03:28,608 --> 00:03:30,008
Yeah, he owes me money.

39
00:03:41,521 --> 00:03:43,990
- No, she didn't.
- Yes, she did.

40
00:04:45,652 --> 00:04:47,211
Hey!

41
00:05:12,012 --> 00:05:13,480
Shut up, man. Shut up.

42
00:05:13,647 --> 00:05:16,207
I got a knife, man, so be still.

43
00:05:16,716 --> 00:05:18,480
Left front pocket.

44
00:05:23,390 --> 00:05:25,222
Ah. Ha, ha.

45
00:05:25,392 --> 00:05:27,384
Very smart.

46
00:05:29,162 --> 00:05:30,562
Let's go.

47
00:05:31,464 --> 00:05:33,296
Help! Help! Help!

48
00:05:33,466 --> 00:05:34,957
Help! Help! Help!

49
00:05:35,135 --> 00:05:36,228
Robbery! Rob...!

50
00:05:36,403 --> 00:05:37,427
Help! Help! Help!

51
00:05:37,604 --> 00:05:38,663
Robbery! Robbery!

52
00:05:38,839 --> 00:05:40,364
Help. Oh, help. Help.

53
00:05:40,540 --> 00:05:42,008
Robbery.

54
00:06:37,097 --> 00:06:38,121
Hey.

55
00:06:38,298 --> 00:06:40,199
- Doggone.
- You got a problem?

56
00:06:40,667 --> 00:06:42,602
If you're trying to rob
me, you're too late.

57
00:06:42,769 --> 00:06:45,432
All I got is the shoes on my
feet, the clothes on my back,

58
00:06:45,605 --> 00:06:46,800
and the fillings in my teeth.

59
00:06:46,973 --> 00:06:49,408
Hey, look, mister, everybody
who lives in this neighborhood

60
00:06:49,576 --> 00:06:50,703
is not out to rob you.

61
00:06:53,446 --> 00:06:55,642
I'm... I'm sorry.

62
00:06:55,815 --> 00:06:57,716
My car broke down,
I just got mugged,

63
00:06:57,884 --> 00:06:59,819
the telephone ate
my last quarter,

64
00:06:59,986 --> 00:07:02,979
and now my barbecue's
probably stone-cold.

65
00:07:03,390 --> 00:07:05,052
That's what you're
doing down here?

66
00:07:05,225 --> 00:07:07,091
Picking up barbeque?

67
00:07:07,327 --> 00:07:10,695
- Red Devil's.
- Best in the world.

68
00:07:11,331 --> 00:07:13,994
So where's this car of yours?

69
00:07:14,167 --> 00:07:15,965
Around the corner.

70
00:07:21,408 --> 00:07:22,899
Just put it in the hole, man.

71
00:07:23,076 --> 00:07:24,601
I'm trying to work this thing.

72
00:07:24,778 --> 00:07:26,440
Hey. Hey!

73
00:07:26,613 --> 00:07:27,911
Hey, get out of there.

74
00:07:28,081 --> 00:07:30,380
Get away! Drop that, hey!

75
00:07:30,717 --> 00:07:32,879
Hey, you guys
heard the man. Move.

76
00:07:33,053 --> 00:07:35,079
Yeah, move, get out of
there. Get out of there.

77
00:07:35,255 --> 00:07:36,883
- Old man.
- Hey, wait a minute.

78
00:07:37,057 --> 00:07:38,992
Hey! Hey, you! That's my bar...

79
00:07:39,693 --> 00:07:40,820
Run...

80
00:07:42,462 --> 00:07:44,454
They got my barbecue.

81
00:07:44,631 --> 00:07:48,159
Like you said, probably
stone-cold anyway.

82
00:07:48,368 --> 00:07:50,166
I could have heated it up.

83
00:07:50,337 --> 00:07:52,169
Not so good then.

84
00:07:52,339 --> 00:07:53,830
Better than nothing.

85
00:07:54,474 --> 00:07:57,308
Ah. Dries it out.

86
00:07:57,877 --> 00:07:59,812
Yeah, I guess.

87
00:08:01,815 --> 00:08:04,307
You know your
barbecue, don't you?

88
00:08:04,584 --> 00:08:05,916
Yep.

89
00:08:06,619 --> 00:08:07,917
Know cars too.

90
00:08:08,822 --> 00:08:11,883
Well, I took a correspondence
course in auto repair.

91
00:08:12,058 --> 00:08:13,890
Last time I was in prison.

92
00:08:17,731 --> 00:08:19,359
Just kidding.

93
00:08:19,566 --> 00:08:21,762
- Stan Johnson.
- Oh, heh-heh.

94
00:08:21,935 --> 00:08:23,870
Ben Matlock. Heh-heh.

95
00:08:26,406 --> 00:08:29,740
God's sake, Ben, what were you
thinking going down there by yourself?

96
00:08:29,909 --> 00:08:33,437
- You could've gotten yourself killed.
- I go there by myself all the time.

97
00:08:33,613 --> 00:08:35,605
It's just that my
car broke down.

98
00:08:35,782 --> 00:08:38,342
- Well, did you tell the police?
- Yeah.

99
00:08:38,518 --> 00:08:42,683
But it happened so fast, I didn't
get a good look at either one of them.

100
00:08:42,856 --> 00:08:45,382
Oh. Oh, wow.

101
00:08:45,558 --> 00:08:48,118
Where were you yesterday?

102
00:08:48,962 --> 00:08:52,057
I called from a pay phone,
but nobody answered.

103
00:08:52,232 --> 00:08:54,758
- Yesterday?
- Yeah. About 5:30.

104
00:08:55,668 --> 00:08:58,638
- Five-thirty? Uh...
- Yeah, you said you'd be here till 6.

105
00:08:58,805 --> 00:09:02,071
And I wasted my last
quarter trying to call you.

106
00:09:02,242 --> 00:09:04,074
By 5:30. Five... Ooh.

107
00:09:04,244 --> 00:09:07,112
Um, it's a real
busy day yesterday.

108
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:10,648
Uh, let me... Let me just
think. Five-thirty was...

109
00:09:10,817 --> 00:09:12,979
- Found him.
- Really?

110
00:09:13,153 --> 00:09:16,715
Mm-hmm. Stanley Johnson. He works
at the Central District Youth Center.

111
00:09:16,890 --> 00:09:18,222
Usually gets there around 9:30.

112
00:09:18,391 --> 00:09:21,088
- Oh, thanks.
- So, what are you gonna do?

113
00:09:21,394 --> 00:09:24,330
I'm gonna go down
there and try to find him.

114
00:09:24,497 --> 00:09:26,523
- Great, great.
- Let's go. Let's go.

115
00:09:28,134 --> 00:09:30,296
Excellent. All right. All
right. Give me the ball now.

116
00:09:30,470 --> 00:09:32,390
Gather around. Let's
see if we can set this shot.

117
00:09:32,539 --> 00:09:34,739
Okay, Bobby, you're gonna
run to pick out Patrick's man.

118
00:09:34,874 --> 00:09:37,139
Maxwell, dish the ball back
out to you, up for the deuce.

119
00:09:37,310 --> 00:09:38,642
- Let's set it up.
- Let's do it.

120
00:09:38,812 --> 00:09:40,508
- Let's go.
- Let's go. Let's go.

121
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:42,012
Come on, baby.

122
00:09:42,182 --> 00:09:43,343
- All right.
- Defense.

123
00:09:43,516 --> 00:09:44,814
- Defense. Defense.
- Go!

124
00:09:44,984 --> 00:09:46,646
- Why do you want it?
- I'm open.

125
00:09:46,820 --> 00:09:49,016
- Let's go.
- Yeah!

126
00:09:49,389 --> 00:09:50,823
Yes. Yes.

127
00:09:50,990 --> 00:09:54,051
Now, try to run it on the
other side. On the other side.

128
00:09:56,830 --> 00:09:58,796
It's a little early for
barbecue, isn't it?

129
00:09:58,820 --> 00:09:59,129
Yeah.

130
00:09:59,299 --> 00:10:01,996
- How you doing?
- Fine.

131
00:10:02,836 --> 00:10:06,000
- Good-looking bunch of boys.
- Yep.

132
00:10:06,172 --> 00:10:09,165
- Shouldn't they be in school?
- Yep.

133
00:10:09,976 --> 00:10:12,104
And if I can keep them
off the streets long enough,

134
00:10:12,278 --> 00:10:13,478
maybe they'll figure that out.

135
00:10:13,646 --> 00:10:15,239
- We got some playground.
- Right away.

136
00:10:15,415 --> 00:10:18,249
- Wayne...
- Here it is, three, two, one.

137
00:10:18,418 --> 00:10:20,284
I wanna thank you.

138
00:10:20,453 --> 00:10:21,751
You did that.

139
00:10:21,921 --> 00:10:23,761
Good, good. All right,
now let's try this play.

140
00:10:24,224 --> 00:10:27,126
Steve, you drive hard from the
hole, dish it up to Calvin for the jumper.

141
00:10:27,293 --> 00:10:28,420
- All right.
- Got it?

142
00:10:28,595 --> 00:10:29,688
All right.

143
00:10:29,863 --> 00:10:32,833
Um, I didn't expect you
to help me out yesterday,

144
00:10:32,999 --> 00:10:36,629
but you did anyway,
and, well, I just...

145
00:10:37,637 --> 00:10:39,037
I wanna pay you back.

146
00:10:39,305 --> 00:10:41,570
What did you say
you did for a living?

147
00:10:41,741 --> 00:10:44,370
- Lawyer.
- Heh-heh.

148
00:10:44,544 --> 00:10:46,376
I know.

149
00:10:49,716 --> 00:10:52,015
- You're a lawyer, huh?
- Yep.

150
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:56,354
Yo, guys, practice free throws
for a while. I'll be right back.

151
00:10:56,523 --> 00:10:59,789
- All right.
- Okay. You got a deal.

152
00:10:59,959 --> 00:11:01,052
- Come on.
- Okay.

153
00:11:03,596 --> 00:11:05,462
- Oh, yeah.
- Oh, yeah.

154
00:11:13,806 --> 00:11:16,139
- You live here?
- Yeah.

155
00:11:16,309 --> 00:11:18,778
Second story, third
apartment from the end.

156
00:11:18,945 --> 00:11:21,847
Come on, you ain't
seen nothing yet.

157
00:11:31,024 --> 00:11:35,257
Supposed to need a key to get in,
but as you can see, lock's broken.

158
00:11:35,428 --> 00:11:37,659
Has been for more than a year.

159
00:11:38,765 --> 00:11:41,064
- It's dark.
- Yep. Lights are burned out.

160
00:11:41,234 --> 00:11:42,998
- How come?
- Landlord.

161
00:11:43,169 --> 00:11:44,501
Never fixes
anything around here.

162
00:11:44,671 --> 00:11:47,106
He feels he doesn't have to.

163
00:11:47,373 --> 00:11:49,239
Of course he has to.

164
00:11:49,409 --> 00:11:54,109
He's legally obligated to
keep this place fit to live in.

165
00:11:54,280 --> 00:11:56,511
I've called the realty
company that owns this dump

166
00:11:56,683 --> 00:11:57,844
at least 30 times.

167
00:11:58,017 --> 00:11:59,610
It's like talking to a wall.

168
00:11:59,786 --> 00:12:02,187
- Call the police?
- Yeah. Said they're looking into it.

169
00:12:02,355 --> 00:12:03,618
That was about six months ago.

170
00:12:03,790 --> 00:12:06,385
- Call the city?
- Said they're looking into it too.

171
00:12:06,559 --> 00:12:08,679
Hell, every bureaucrat
in this city's looking into it,

172
00:12:08,828 --> 00:12:10,592
but nothing ever happens.

173
00:12:10,763 --> 00:12:13,232
- Why don't you move?
- I could.

174
00:12:13,399 --> 00:12:15,439
I could move out of this
neighborhood, of this city,

175
00:12:15,602 --> 00:12:19,300
out of this life if I wanted
to, but I don't want to.

176
00:12:19,505 --> 00:12:23,101
Something's gotta be
done, so I guess I'm it.

177
00:12:24,844 --> 00:12:27,404
Sounds like you need a lawyer.

178
00:12:27,914 --> 00:12:30,110
Yeah, it does, doesn't it?

179
00:12:39,959 --> 00:12:41,951
I'm pretty sure the
Drake Apartments

180
00:12:42,128 --> 00:12:44,620
is one of Mr. Mitchell's
properties. Yes.

181
00:12:44,797 --> 00:12:48,131
- Mr. Mitchell?
- George Mitchell.

182
00:12:48,301 --> 00:12:51,362
George Mitchell's
about 6'2", balding,

183
00:12:51,537 --> 00:12:53,369
a member of the
Atlanta Charities Board?

184
00:12:53,539 --> 00:12:55,007
Mm-hmm. Yes, that's him.

185
00:12:55,575 --> 00:12:57,703
Well, tell him I'm
here. I wanna see him.

186
00:12:57,877 --> 00:12:59,311
My name's Ben Matlock.

187
00:12:59,479 --> 00:13:01,175
Mm-hmm.

188
00:13:02,482 --> 00:13:06,613
Yes, there's a Ben Matlock
out here would like to see you.

189
00:13:07,820 --> 00:13:10,051
Ben, what are you doing here?

190
00:13:10,223 --> 00:13:13,716
- Could I talk with you for a minute?
- Oh, sure, come on in.

191
00:13:20,300 --> 00:13:21,859
Don't tell me I missed
another meeting.

192
00:13:22,035 --> 00:13:25,699
Oh, no, this doesn't have anything
to do with the charities board.

193
00:13:28,141 --> 00:13:30,940
What? What are you staring at?

194
00:13:31,210 --> 00:13:33,236
Your desk.

195
00:13:33,413 --> 00:13:37,942
I got so much stuff piled on
my desk, I lose things for days.

196
00:13:38,117 --> 00:13:41,349
Oh, heh-heh. So,
what is this about?

197
00:13:43,489 --> 00:13:47,358
Uh, it's about that apartment building
you own down in the Central District.

198
00:13:47,527 --> 00:13:49,018
The Drake Apartments.

199
00:13:49,362 --> 00:13:50,386
Yes?

200
00:13:50,563 --> 00:13:53,123
I had occasion to visit
somebody down there,

201
00:13:53,299 --> 00:13:57,066
and I was just appalled by
the condition of that building.

202
00:13:57,737 --> 00:13:59,638
There's broken glass everywhere,

203
00:13:59,806 --> 00:14:01,502
there's no lights in the hall,

204
00:14:01,674 --> 00:14:04,473
there's trash all
over the place.

205
00:14:04,644 --> 00:14:07,739
If you went down, I'm sure
you'd be just as appalled.

206
00:14:07,914 --> 00:14:09,746
I was just down there last week.

207
00:14:10,783 --> 00:14:13,184
And you didn't do
anything about it?

208
00:14:13,519 --> 00:14:14,817
About what?

209
00:14:16,089 --> 00:14:18,024
Well, I mean, granted,
it's not the Taj Mahal,

210
00:14:18,191 --> 00:14:20,160
but it's certainly
better than nothing.

211
00:14:20,827 --> 00:14:21,920
I don't get it.

212
00:14:22,095 --> 00:14:24,826
Oh. Ben, do you know how much

213
00:14:24,997 --> 00:14:27,831
a two-bedroom apartment
down there goes for?

214
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,299
Two hundred ten dollars a month.

215
00:14:30,470 --> 00:14:31,802
I mean, really.

216
00:14:31,971 --> 00:14:35,430
I'll bet your gas bill for the month
of January was higher than that.

217
00:14:35,608 --> 00:14:36,906
Now, as far as I'm concerned,

218
00:14:37,076 --> 00:14:39,944
I'm doing this community
a public service.

219
00:14:40,446 --> 00:14:42,779
There are rats in the halls.

220
00:14:42,949 --> 00:14:45,441
That's because those people
leave their garbage in the halls.

221
00:14:45,618 --> 00:14:48,178
If you'd fix the trash chute
so they could use that...

222
00:14:48,354 --> 00:14:50,255
Ben, Ben, please
try to understand.

223
00:14:50,656 --> 00:14:53,216
How I choose to
manage my property

224
00:14:53,393 --> 00:14:56,022
is really none of your business.

225
00:15:02,335 --> 00:15:04,804
A friend of mine
wants to sue you.

226
00:15:05,838 --> 00:15:09,468
Does the name Stan
Johnson ring a bell?

227
00:15:10,410 --> 00:15:13,539
Since when did you
start representing renters?

228
00:15:13,846 --> 00:15:18,307
Be at his place
tomorrow morning, 9:00.

229
00:15:19,018 --> 00:15:20,782
I'm busy tomorrow morning.

230
00:15:20,953 --> 00:15:23,184
He's gonna sue you.

231
00:15:25,224 --> 00:15:27,750
Be there, 9:00.

232
00:15:28,127 --> 00:15:30,790
He's got a good case.

233
00:15:40,673 --> 00:15:42,767
- I got your popcorn popper.
- All right.

234
00:15:43,476 --> 00:15:45,342
Here it is. I cleaned
it and everything.

235
00:15:45,511 --> 00:15:47,241
You want me to put
it back in the kitchen?

236
00:15:47,413 --> 00:15:48,676
Okay.

237
00:15:49,182 --> 00:15:51,310
So I might be
needing it again soon.

238
00:15:52,618 --> 00:15:54,109
- Okay.
- Ah.

239
00:15:54,287 --> 00:15:56,756
- Could you do me a favor?
- What?

240
00:15:56,923 --> 00:16:00,052
The cable company's sending
a crew over here on the 27th

241
00:16:00,226 --> 00:16:01,455
to put in a new box,

242
00:16:01,627 --> 00:16:04,119
and I can't be here because
I got hearings all day.

243
00:16:04,297 --> 00:16:06,289
Could you hang
around and let them in?

244
00:16:07,567 --> 00:16:10,765
- The 27th?
- Yeah, between 2 and 6.

245
00:16:10,937 --> 00:16:13,031
- Nope. I can't.
- What do you mean you can't do it?

246
00:16:13,206 --> 00:16:15,038
- Can't do it.
- Why?

247
00:16:15,675 --> 00:16:17,234
I just can't.

248
00:16:17,410 --> 00:16:18,844
Get somebody else.

249
00:16:19,278 --> 00:16:21,577
I do favors for
you all the time.

250
00:16:21,747 --> 00:16:24,410
Defend your boy, loan
you my popcorn popper,

251
00:16:24,584 --> 00:16:26,382
and I ask you to
do one little thing.

252
00:16:26,552 --> 00:16:29,386
I've done lots of things
for you plenty of times.

253
00:16:29,555 --> 00:16:32,582
- Like what?
- Lots of things.

254
00:16:32,758 --> 00:16:34,750
Plenty of times.

255
00:16:35,061 --> 00:16:36,495
Here.

256
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:42,132
Jackass.

257
00:16:42,301 --> 00:16:44,429
I heard that!

258
00:17:03,089 --> 00:17:04,955
Hello, George.

259
00:17:05,491 --> 00:17:08,757
- Stan Johnson, George Mitchell.
- Hi, we talked on the phone once.

260
00:17:08,928 --> 00:17:10,863
If we could do this
quickly, I'd appreciate it.

261
00:17:11,030 --> 00:17:12,054
Okay.

262
00:17:12,231 --> 00:17:15,065
I can see it. I'm not blind.

263
00:17:20,339 --> 00:17:22,774
I suppose this is my fault?

264
00:17:23,042 --> 00:17:24,601
Well, if the lock
on the door worked,

265
00:17:24,777 --> 00:17:26,939
maybe he wouldn't
be able to get in here.

266
00:17:27,113 --> 00:17:29,048
Excuse me.

267
00:17:29,482 --> 00:17:31,576
Hollis. Come on,
up and at them, man.

268
00:17:36,122 --> 00:17:39,615
- No, no, no, this way.
- Oh, yeah, yeah.

269
00:17:41,427 --> 00:17:44,829
Here you go, man, here
you go. Go get some coffee.

270
00:17:53,272 --> 00:17:54,706
Friend of yours?

271
00:17:58,377 --> 00:18:01,211
Let's show him Gordon's place.

272
00:18:02,615 --> 00:18:04,743
Gordon Matheny.
Nice to meet you.

273
00:18:05,084 --> 00:18:06,780
Can I get you anything?

274
00:18:06,953 --> 00:18:08,785
No, thank you.

275
00:18:09,522 --> 00:18:11,616
Gordon's lived here
almost ten years.

276
00:18:11,791 --> 00:18:14,283
He's always paid his
rent in full and on time.

277
00:18:14,460 --> 00:18:15,928
But not lately.

278
00:18:16,095 --> 00:18:19,190
Gordon, why don't you tell him why
you haven't been paying your rent?

279
00:18:20,199 --> 00:18:23,431
I'm just fed up with how nothing
ever gets done around here.

280
00:18:23,603 --> 00:18:25,265
Things never get fixed.

281
00:18:25,438 --> 00:18:26,599
Yeah.

282
00:18:26,772 --> 00:18:28,297
Like this?

283
00:18:28,808 --> 00:18:30,401
Look at this. Torn up carpet.

284
00:18:31,577 --> 00:18:34,672
Look at these walls. They need
to be re-scraped and painted.

285
00:18:35,448 --> 00:18:37,178
And look at this.

286
00:18:37,350 --> 00:18:39,285
This was like this
when he moved in here.

287
00:18:41,587 --> 00:18:43,215
Follow me.

288
00:18:52,098 --> 00:18:54,829
No water. He has to
wash up in the kitchen,

289
00:18:55,001 --> 00:18:57,163
the plumbing in here's
been busted for nine months.

290
00:18:57,336 --> 00:18:59,635
If you people were more
careful about what you put down...

291
00:18:59,805 --> 00:19:01,068
"You people"?

292
00:19:01,240 --> 00:19:03,505
And just who do you
mean by "you people"?

293
00:19:04,210 --> 00:19:05,803
You tenants.

294
00:19:05,978 --> 00:19:07,776
The people who are
causing this property

295
00:19:07,947 --> 00:19:09,973
to plummet in value
even as we speak.

296
00:19:10,149 --> 00:19:12,050
Well, isn't that why
you bought this place?

297
00:19:12,218 --> 00:19:14,418
So that you could write off
the depreciation every year?

298
00:19:14,553 --> 00:19:17,318
The point is, why should I
put good money into something

299
00:19:17,490 --> 00:19:19,459
you tenants are just
gonna trash anyway?

300
00:19:19,625 --> 00:19:22,151
Well, maybe if you took
better care of this place,

301
00:19:22,328 --> 00:19:24,354
this place wouldn't
get so trashed.

302
00:19:24,530 --> 00:19:27,694
Man, you don't even think
enough of me to shake my hand.

303
00:19:28,334 --> 00:19:30,200
You're breaking the law, George.

304
00:19:30,369 --> 00:19:32,668
Wait, wait, wait a minute.
I'm breaking the law?

305
00:19:32,838 --> 00:19:35,706
This man in here just admitted
he hasn't been paying his rent.

306
00:19:35,875 --> 00:19:38,037
You're in the wrong,
you're breaking the law.

307
00:19:38,210 --> 00:19:41,180
If I have to drag you into
court to prove it, I'll do it.

308
00:19:50,923 --> 00:19:52,323
- I called them.
- Did they call?

309
00:19:52,491 --> 00:19:54,611
- They can be there.
- Excellent. Now, what about him?

310
00:19:54,760 --> 00:19:57,491
- No, I can't get ahold of him. Call him.
- All right, I'll try.

311
00:19:57,663 --> 00:19:58,824
- Hi.
- Hey, pop.

312
00:19:58,998 --> 00:20:02,059
- Hi.
- So far 37 people are gonna come.

313
00:20:02,234 --> 00:20:04,863
And we can start
decorating the room at 4 p.m.

314
00:20:05,037 --> 00:20:06,903
Yeah, I got the
food all ordered.

315
00:20:07,073 --> 00:20:09,508
Great. Now you sure you got
everything? Hot dogs, chips,

316
00:20:09,675 --> 00:20:12,577
- cake, punch, uh, hors d'oeuvres?
- Yeah.

317
00:20:12,745 --> 00:20:14,543
Gotta make more popcorn.

318
00:20:14,714 --> 00:20:16,683
Enough for 45 people.

319
00:20:17,349 --> 00:20:20,114
Forty-four. I'm not going.

320
00:20:20,286 --> 00:20:21,982
- You're not going?
- Why not?

321
00:20:22,154 --> 00:20:23,383
He called me a jackass.

322
00:20:23,856 --> 00:20:26,826
- You call him a jackass all the time.
- I know.

323
00:20:26,992 --> 00:20:28,016
What's the big deal?

324
00:20:28,327 --> 00:20:31,855
He called me a jackass
close to his birthday.

325
00:20:32,665 --> 00:20:34,258
He must mean it.

326
00:20:43,776 --> 00:20:46,075
- Are you Gordon Matheny?
- Yes.

327
00:20:46,245 --> 00:20:48,214
I got an order of eviction
here, Mr. Matheny.

328
00:20:48,380 --> 00:20:51,373
I'm afraid you're gonna have to
vacate the premises immediately.

329
00:20:55,087 --> 00:20:56,646
You're throwing me out?

330
00:20:56,822 --> 00:21:00,315
You were notified by mail that
you owed four months back rent,

331
00:21:00,493 --> 00:21:03,156
and that failure to pay
will result in eviction.

332
00:21:03,763 --> 00:21:06,164
But where am I supposed to go?

333
00:21:06,332 --> 00:21:08,927
And what am I supposed
to do with all my stuff?

334
00:21:09,101 --> 00:21:12,560
Look, all I know is it's
gotta be out of here. Now.

335
00:21:17,409 --> 00:21:19,241
Would you just tell him
Stan Johnson's here?

336
00:21:19,411 --> 00:21:20,640
He knows who I am.

337
00:21:20,813 --> 00:21:23,647
Well, I'm afraid
Mr. Mitchell's not in right now.

338
00:21:23,816 --> 00:21:27,275
I saw his car in the
parking lot. The man's here.

339
00:21:27,453 --> 00:21:29,388
He's in a meeting.

340
00:21:29,688 --> 00:21:31,384
Then get him out of it.

341
00:21:31,557 --> 00:21:34,322
Look, lady, because of him, an old
man's being thrown out of his home.

342
00:21:34,493 --> 00:21:36,860
- It's an emergency.
- I'll have him call you.

343
00:21:37,029 --> 00:21:39,863
No, I wanna see him now.

344
00:21:40,032 --> 00:21:41,967
He's not going to
see you, Mr. Johnson.

345
00:21:42,134 --> 00:21:46,230
And I'm afraid if you don't leave,
I'm going to have to call the police.

346
00:21:48,140 --> 00:21:51,907
Sooner or later, I'll see him.

347
00:22:09,628 --> 00:22:11,597
Help me!

348
00:22:17,002 --> 00:22:19,836
Somebody. Somebody help me!

349
00:22:36,322 --> 00:22:39,884
- Man, you all right?
- Please.

350
00:22:56,642 --> 00:22:58,201
Somebody jumped me from behind,

351
00:22:58,377 --> 00:23:00,937
pulled me into one of
those demolished buildings,

352
00:23:01,113 --> 00:23:02,706
started going at
me with a knife.

353
00:23:02,882 --> 00:23:04,043
Did you get a look at him?

354
00:23:04,216 --> 00:23:06,014
I mean, what the hell
kind of world is this?

355
00:23:06,185 --> 00:23:08,051
You can't even
walk down the street.

356
00:23:08,220 --> 00:23:10,655
Sir, can you describe
the assailant?

357
00:23:11,423 --> 00:23:15,053
He had his arm around my
neck. I couldn't turn around.

358
00:23:15,427 --> 00:23:16,827
Get a look at his clothes?

359
00:23:17,429 --> 00:23:21,332
Just the, uh, sleeve of
his shirt. It was flannel.

360
00:23:21,500 --> 00:23:25,631
Either, uh, black and green
or blue and green checks.

361
00:23:26,071 --> 00:23:27,733
- He say anything?
- No.

362
00:23:27,907 --> 00:23:30,843
He just, uh, held on

363
00:23:31,010 --> 00:23:33,343
and kept trying to stab me.

364
00:23:35,648 --> 00:23:38,208
Is it too much to ask to
be safe on the streets?

365
00:23:39,919 --> 00:23:42,411
In that neighborhood, maybe.

366
00:23:42,588 --> 00:23:44,108
What in the world
were you doing there?

367
00:23:44,623 --> 00:23:47,457
Oh, my company owns the
Drake apartment building.

368
00:23:47,626 --> 00:23:48,924
We had to evict somebody today.

369
00:23:49,094 --> 00:23:50,722
I just wanted to make
sure he was gone.

370
00:23:50,896 --> 00:23:53,627
What was his name?
Do you remember?

371
00:24:10,482 --> 00:24:13,042
- Stan Johnson?
- Yeah.

372
00:24:13,218 --> 00:24:17,053
Detective Robert Garrison. Like
to ask you a couple of questions.

373
00:24:20,893 --> 00:24:22,862
Just got done talking
to Gordon Matheny

374
00:24:23,028 --> 00:24:24,553
over at the South
Street Shelter.

375
00:24:24,730 --> 00:24:25,925
Told us when he got evicted,

376
00:24:26,098 --> 00:24:28,067
you were more upset
about it than he was.

377
00:24:28,233 --> 00:24:30,293
Well, yeah, I was mad as hell.

378
00:24:30,469 --> 00:24:32,597
The guy's 73 years
old, for God's sakes.

379
00:24:33,405 --> 00:24:36,204
George Mitchell was attacked
in an alley up the block tonight.

380
00:24:36,375 --> 00:24:39,072
- You hear about that?
- No.

381
00:24:39,244 --> 00:24:41,645
Yeah. Somebody
tried to slice him up.

382
00:24:41,814 --> 00:24:44,374
Didn't go for his watch or
his wallet, just went for him.

383
00:24:44,550 --> 00:24:47,418
Turns out Mr. Matheny was
eating dinner over at the shelter

384
00:24:47,586 --> 00:24:48,781
when all this was going on.

385
00:24:48,954 --> 00:24:52,083
Besides, like you
said, he's an old man.

386
00:24:52,257 --> 00:24:54,257
Too old to be dragging
people off, cutting them up.

387
00:24:54,426 --> 00:24:55,466
- What's your point?
- Sir?

388
00:24:55,627 --> 00:24:57,789
- Excuse me.
- Take a look at this.

389
00:25:09,141 --> 00:25:11,303
- This your shirt?
- Yeah.

390
00:25:11,477 --> 00:25:13,002
What's this on the sleeve?

391
00:25:13,178 --> 00:25:14,510
I don't know.

392
00:25:14,680 --> 00:25:16,808
Looks to me like blood.

393
00:25:29,928 --> 00:25:33,660
You should never have gone
up to his office without me.

394
00:25:33,832 --> 00:25:35,425
I know.

395
00:25:35,834 --> 00:25:37,359
Guess I really
need a lawyer now.

396
00:25:38,637 --> 00:25:41,334
Yeah. They'll
charge you tomorrow.

397
00:25:41,507 --> 00:25:42,702
Attempted murder.

398
00:25:43,242 --> 00:25:46,508
- I didn't do it, Ben.
- Oh, I know.

399
00:25:46,678 --> 00:25:48,909
I have to ask you this stuff.

400
00:25:49,081 --> 00:25:52,051
Where were you
tonight about 7:30?

401
00:25:52,418 --> 00:25:55,445
Just home. Watching
TV, same as always.

402
00:25:56,655 --> 00:25:58,453
Working with those
kids all day, Ben,

403
00:25:58,624 --> 00:25:59,717
they wipe me out.

404
00:25:59,892 --> 00:26:02,054
I come home, I take a shower,

405
00:26:02,227 --> 00:26:04,719
I turn on the tube
and just vegetate.

406
00:26:05,130 --> 00:26:07,190
Yeah, I imagine.

407
00:26:07,366 --> 00:26:10,734
So, did you...? Did you
call anybody or anybody visit

408
00:26:10,903 --> 00:26:14,396
or is there any way to verify
you were home all night?

409
00:26:17,876 --> 00:26:19,469
No.

410
00:26:20,546 --> 00:26:23,539
The police have done
some preliminary studies.

411
00:26:23,715 --> 00:26:26,082
The stain on
your shirt is blood.

412
00:26:26,251 --> 00:26:28,049
Same type as George Mitchell's.

413
00:26:29,354 --> 00:26:31,949
I have no idea how it
got there, Ben. I swear.

414
00:26:32,124 --> 00:26:33,844
That shirt, it's been
lying around for days.

415
00:26:33,992 --> 00:26:36,154
I left it there after I wore
it playing basketball.

416
00:26:40,299 --> 00:26:41,892
That's gonna be
a problem, isn't it?

417
00:26:43,869 --> 00:26:47,704
Well, we'll give
it our best shot.

418
00:26:58,884 --> 00:27:01,012
- Any more coffee?
- Oh, you betcha.

419
00:27:01,186 --> 00:27:02,984
I'll get you a cup.

420
00:27:04,723 --> 00:27:06,988
So, what time's the hearing?

421
00:27:07,426 --> 00:27:09,156
Nine-thirty.

422
00:27:11,964 --> 00:27:15,196
- Did he do it, Ben?
- No.

423
00:27:15,367 --> 00:27:16,733
He's mad at George Mitchell,

424
00:27:16,902 --> 00:27:18,336
but he didn't cut
him up like that.

425
00:27:18,504 --> 00:27:20,996
Though I'd almost
understand if he did.

426
00:27:21,173 --> 00:27:22,368
Why?

427
00:27:22,541 --> 00:27:24,635
Oh, you ought to see
that apartment building.

428
00:27:24,810 --> 00:27:29,741
It's so dark, and
dirty, depressing.

429
00:27:29,915 --> 00:27:33,613
How can he expect his tenants
to have respect for his property

430
00:27:33,785 --> 00:27:36,584
when he obviously
has no respect for them?

431
00:27:36,755 --> 00:27:40,556
It's wrong to make
people live like that.

432
00:27:42,528 --> 00:27:44,656
You should buy the
place and fix it up.

433
00:27:44,830 --> 00:27:47,459
He'd never sell. He
needs it as a tax write-off.

434
00:27:47,633 --> 00:27:49,192
Oh.

435
00:27:50,602 --> 00:27:53,128
- But you know what?
- Hmm?

436
00:27:53,305 --> 00:27:54,637
We could fix it up a little.

437
00:27:54,806 --> 00:27:56,365
You and me and
Cliff and the tenants.

438
00:27:56,542 --> 00:27:58,238
Get some material,
paint and stuff.

439
00:27:58,410 --> 00:28:00,675
- We've got the manpower.
- Yeah.

440
00:28:00,846 --> 00:28:02,371
Oh, yeah.

441
00:28:04,082 --> 00:28:07,416
Your points are well made
and well taken, Mr. Matlock.

442
00:28:07,586 --> 00:28:09,953
But I would be remiss if I
didn't take the viciousness

443
00:28:10,122 --> 00:28:13,991
of the attack of which he stands
accused into consideration as well.

444
00:28:14,159 --> 00:28:16,856
Bail is set at $100,000.

445
00:28:17,029 --> 00:28:18,122
Next case.

446
00:28:21,400 --> 00:28:23,494
- Case number...
- I'll take care of the bail.

447
00:28:23,669 --> 00:28:26,229
- No, I can't let you do that.
- It's only 10 percent.

448
00:28:26,405 --> 00:28:29,432
There's no risk of flight.
It's the least I can do.

449
00:28:32,778 --> 00:28:35,179
Do you make it a habit of
putting up bail for your clients?

450
00:28:35,347 --> 00:28:37,543
I can count them on one hand.

451
00:28:37,716 --> 00:28:40,845
- I gotta make a pit stop. Wait for me.
- Okay.

452
00:28:49,027 --> 00:28:51,121
Mr. Mitchell. Mr. Mitchell,
would you wait a minute?

453
00:28:51,296 --> 00:28:53,026
What do you want?

454
00:28:53,198 --> 00:28:54,564
I didn't do this to you.

455
00:28:55,133 --> 00:28:57,728
I didn't accuse you. Excuse me.

456
00:28:57,903 --> 00:28:59,633
No, wait, come on.

457
00:29:00,272 --> 00:29:02,264
Let me finish.

458
00:29:04,476 --> 00:29:06,377
I don't like the way that
you run your business,

459
00:29:06,545 --> 00:29:07,843
and I don't like you.

460
00:29:08,647 --> 00:29:10,548
But I would never
do this to you.

461
00:29:11,183 --> 00:29:13,015
Or anyone else.

462
00:29:14,052 --> 00:29:15,680
Thank you.

463
00:29:34,673 --> 00:29:37,643
- Well, I got a little more paint.
- Oh, good.

464
00:29:37,809 --> 00:29:39,710
Yeah. Ah.

465
00:29:39,878 --> 00:29:41,813
Hello, Ben.

466
00:29:43,448 --> 00:29:45,178
Oh, hello, Billy.

467
00:29:45,484 --> 00:29:47,715
Okay if I help out?

468
00:29:48,553 --> 00:29:50,920
The more the merrier.

469
00:29:51,089 --> 00:29:53,752
Borrowed your paint
brush. Do you mind?

470
00:29:54,393 --> 00:29:56,225
That's okay.

471
00:29:57,396 --> 00:30:00,833
Uh, the cable people are
coming to install a new box

472
00:30:00,999 --> 00:30:02,433
at the house on the 27th,

473
00:30:03,201 --> 00:30:04,726
and since I can't be there

474
00:30:04,903 --> 00:30:07,600
and Billy couldn't
arrange to be there,

475
00:30:07,773 --> 00:30:10,834
could you be there between
2 and 6 to let them in?

476
00:30:11,009 --> 00:30:12,602
Sure. I'd be glad to.

477
00:30:12,778 --> 00:30:14,508
Thanks.

478
00:30:16,548 --> 00:30:19,108
You can't do that. You got
a party to go to. Remember?

479
00:30:19,284 --> 00:30:23,346
I'll just call the cable company
and tell them to come another time.

480
00:30:23,522 --> 00:30:25,582
Why didn't I think of that?

481
00:30:26,124 --> 00:30:28,252
You're good at
this sneaky stuff.

482
00:30:28,427 --> 00:30:30,396
It's a gift.

483
00:30:31,663 --> 00:30:33,689
Looking good.

484
00:30:41,606 --> 00:30:43,575
What's going on here?

485
00:30:43,742 --> 00:30:45,734
Heh. Hollis. You know what,

486
00:30:45,911 --> 00:30:47,971
you sleep here so much,
you're practically a tenant.

487
00:30:48,146 --> 00:30:49,444
So get to work.

488
00:30:49,815 --> 00:30:51,613
Oh, come on,
Stan. All I wanna do

489
00:30:51,783 --> 00:30:54,309
is just get by the radiator
so I can have my dinner.

490
00:30:54,486 --> 00:30:56,955
Just what color would
dinner be tonight?

491
00:30:57,122 --> 00:30:59,887
Oh, lookie, a modest,
but amusing red.

492
00:31:00,058 --> 00:31:02,027
- Isn't that nice?
- Oh, come on.

493
00:31:12,404 --> 00:31:14,635
Heh. How you doing?

494
00:31:14,806 --> 00:31:16,968
- Hi.
- Hi.

495
00:31:17,509 --> 00:31:19,478
I'm Cliff.

496
00:31:19,644 --> 00:31:22,375
Uh, do you come
here every night?

497
00:31:22,647 --> 00:31:24,240
Just about.

498
00:31:24,416 --> 00:31:26,248
- About this same time?
- Mm-hmm.

499
00:31:26,418 --> 00:31:28,148
Were you here two nights ago?

500
00:31:28,320 --> 00:31:30,118
Uh, two night...? Yeah. Mm-hmm.

501
00:31:30,389 --> 00:31:33,518
You came about this same time
and you stayed the whole night?

502
00:31:34,826 --> 00:31:36,488
The whole night.

503
00:31:43,001 --> 00:31:45,061
Not a good idea, Cliff.

504
00:31:46,104 --> 00:31:48,232
Do you have a better idea?

505
00:31:53,545 --> 00:31:55,810
I didn't think so.

506
00:32:04,923 --> 00:32:08,382
Uh, would you tell
us where you were

507
00:32:08,560 --> 00:32:10,586
on the night of the
third, Mr. Greeley?

508
00:32:10,762 --> 00:32:14,529
Yes, sir. At 7:00, I was
at the Drake Apartments

509
00:32:14,800 --> 00:32:17,634
in the hallway there
by the radiators.

510
00:32:17,803 --> 00:32:19,829
I sat down like I always do.

511
00:32:20,005 --> 00:32:21,701
Uh-huh. When'd you leave?

512
00:32:21,873 --> 00:32:24,069
Uh, the next morning.
I stayed there all night.

513
00:32:24,443 --> 00:32:26,708
Did you see Stan
Johnson that night?

514
00:32:27,746 --> 00:32:30,773
Oh, yeah, he came
in, uh, right after me.

515
00:32:30,949 --> 00:32:34,181
Oh, so Stan entered the
building a little after 7?

516
00:32:34,352 --> 00:32:35,376
Yes, sir.

517
00:32:35,554 --> 00:32:37,887
And did you see him
leave the building that night?

518
00:32:38,056 --> 00:32:39,615
Oh, no. No, he didn't leave.

519
00:32:39,791 --> 00:32:43,023
And I sat right there by
that radiator all night long.

520
00:32:43,195 --> 00:32:45,960
- Thank you. That's all.
- Thank you.

521
00:32:47,866 --> 00:32:49,300
Stay right there, Mr. Greeley.

522
00:32:54,840 --> 00:32:58,072
You spend a lot of nights
sleeping by the radiator

523
00:32:58,243 --> 00:33:00,439
- at the Drake Apartments, don't you?
- Yes, sir.

524
00:33:00,612 --> 00:33:03,776
- Do you have a home?
- Not at the moment.

525
00:33:03,949 --> 00:33:06,384
How often do you see the
defendant, Stan Johnson,

526
00:33:06,551 --> 00:33:09,043
- when you spend the night there?
- Practically all the time.

527
00:33:09,221 --> 00:33:12,350
He comes in right
after 7, just after me.

528
00:33:12,524 --> 00:33:14,015
Practically all the time.

529
00:33:14,192 --> 00:33:16,252
Does that mean sometimes
you don't see him?

530
00:33:17,162 --> 00:33:19,358
Well, I don't see
him all the time, no.

531
00:33:19,531 --> 00:33:22,399
Then how can you be so sure that
you saw him on the night of the third?

532
00:33:22,567 --> 00:33:26,299
- Well, heh-heh, I just am, that's all.
- Mmm.

533
00:33:28,173 --> 00:33:29,971
Do you drink, Mr. Greeley?

534
00:33:31,109 --> 00:33:32,304
Socially.

535
00:33:32,477 --> 00:33:35,311
And were you drinking
on the night of the third?

536
00:33:37,082 --> 00:33:39,642
Just a... Just a little bit.

537
00:33:39,818 --> 00:33:41,514
Who else came into
the building that night?

538
00:33:41,686 --> 00:33:43,848
- Couple of people.
- What were their names?

539
00:33:44,022 --> 00:33:46,958
I don't... I don't know
their names, I mean...

540
00:33:47,125 --> 00:33:49,594
Why is it that you know
Stan Johnson by name

541
00:33:49,761 --> 00:33:51,525
and you don't know
these other people?

542
00:33:51,696 --> 00:33:53,256
Well, because Stan
is always nice to me.

543
00:33:53,398 --> 00:33:56,391
Always says hi and gives me a
little money every now and then

544
00:33:56,568 --> 00:34:01,029
for, uh, coffee and
doughnuts and stuff like that.

545
00:34:01,206 --> 00:34:03,539
He gives you money.

546
00:34:03,708 --> 00:34:05,540
How much money?

547
00:34:05,710 --> 00:34:09,477
Oh, I don't know. A
dollar here, a dollar there.

548
00:34:09,648 --> 00:34:11,310
How often does he
give you this money?

549
00:34:11,483 --> 00:34:13,349
I just... Hard to say.

550
00:34:13,518 --> 00:34:16,454
You don't always use it for
coffee, do you, Mr. Greeley?

551
00:34:19,424 --> 00:34:21,416
Sometimes you use it for Scotch

552
00:34:21,593 --> 00:34:23,721
or is it a little cheap wine?

553
00:34:23,895 --> 00:34:26,126
I mean, how often do
you drink, Mr. Greeley?

554
00:34:26,298 --> 00:34:28,392
Whenever you get some
money from Mr. Johnson?

555
00:34:29,901 --> 00:34:31,836
- But...
- Isn't that why you wanted

556
00:34:32,003 --> 00:34:33,835
to testify on his behalf
in the first place?

557
00:34:34,005 --> 00:34:35,803
Because of the things
that he does for you?

558
00:34:35,974 --> 00:34:38,307
And in view of the fact
that, by your own admission,

559
00:34:38,476 --> 00:34:41,275
you were under the influence of
alcohol on the night of the attack,

560
00:34:41,446 --> 00:34:43,938
isn't it possible,
if not highly likely,

561
00:34:44,115 --> 00:34:47,745
that you didn't see Stan Johnson
enter the building at 7:00 that night?

562
00:34:47,919 --> 00:34:50,548
That you have confused
it with another night

563
00:34:50,722 --> 00:34:53,055
or that he actually
came in the building

564
00:34:53,224 --> 00:34:55,420
at 8 or 9:00 that night?

565
00:34:56,828 --> 00:34:58,524
Could you repeat the question?

566
00:34:59,564 --> 00:35:00,998
I'll rephrase it.

567
00:35:01,166 --> 00:35:03,829
I know that you feel
indebted to Stan Johnson.

568
00:35:04,002 --> 00:35:06,733
And I understand that
you wanna repay him

569
00:35:06,905 --> 00:35:08,100
by giving him an alibi,

570
00:35:09,140 --> 00:35:12,269
but isn't it true, Mr. Greeley, that
the only thing you can say for sure

571
00:35:12,444 --> 00:35:16,211
you saw that night is the
bottom of a wine bottle?

572
00:35:19,818 --> 00:35:21,810
No further questions.

573
00:35:27,759 --> 00:35:29,728
- Here you go.
- Mm-hmm.

574
00:35:36,501 --> 00:35:37,867
Hey.

575
00:35:38,036 --> 00:35:39,698
- Anything?
- No.

576
00:35:39,871 --> 00:35:42,238
Nobody in the 'hood saw
anything, found anything,

577
00:35:42,407 --> 00:35:45,206
heard anything, knows anything.

578
00:35:45,443 --> 00:35:47,412
We're never gonna
find the guy who did it.

579
00:35:47,579 --> 00:35:49,047
Well,

580
00:35:50,849 --> 00:35:53,580
we'll just have to
convince the jury

581
00:35:53,752 --> 00:35:55,880
that Stan didn't do it.

582
00:35:56,054 --> 00:35:59,547
Yeah, well, if we can just
figure out how that blood

583
00:35:59,724 --> 00:36:02,455
got on George Mitchell's
shirt, we'd be home free.

584
00:36:02,627 --> 00:36:05,495
- Let me ask you something.
- Yeah?

585
00:36:05,964 --> 00:36:10,228
I saw the man's
desk, and it was clean.

586
00:36:10,402 --> 00:36:11,426
I'm telling you clean.

587
00:36:11,603 --> 00:36:14,038
There wasn't a pen, a
pencil, a piece of paper,

588
00:36:14,205 --> 00:36:17,505
anything on that
desk, but a telephone.

589
00:36:17,676 --> 00:36:21,272
And I watched him when he was
down at that tenement building of his.

590
00:36:21,446 --> 00:36:23,677
He had on a nice suit

591
00:36:24,149 --> 00:36:27,119
and an old beat-up
pair of shoes.

592
00:36:27,285 --> 00:36:30,847
Bet you anything he threw
them away afterwards.

593
00:36:31,022 --> 00:36:32,547
Kept his hands in his pockets.

594
00:36:32,724 --> 00:36:35,990
Didn't shake hands with
anybody. He didn't touch anything.

595
00:36:36,261 --> 00:36:39,561
And, uh, during
the bail hearing,

596
00:36:39,731 --> 00:36:43,532
I saw him wash his
hands two times in an hour.

597
00:36:43,702 --> 00:36:47,195
- The man has a clean fetish.
- Yeah, it sounds like it.

598
00:36:47,372 --> 00:36:52,037
Now, what's a man
like that doing in a dirty,

599
00:36:52,210 --> 00:36:57,345
trash-filled, awful
falling-down building

600
00:36:57,515 --> 00:36:59,882
in the middle of
the night by himself?

601
00:37:00,051 --> 00:37:02,646
On top of that, you know what,
he parked his car two blocks away

602
00:37:02,821 --> 00:37:04,483
he was afraid it was
gonna get ripped off.

603
00:37:05,223 --> 00:37:08,352
- I don't believe that for a second.
- Mmm.

604
00:37:13,364 --> 00:37:18,029
Mr. Mitchell, will you please
tell the court what this is?

605
00:37:18,403 --> 00:37:21,066
It appears to be a copy of
the emergency room report

606
00:37:21,239 --> 00:37:23,174
that was filed the
night I was attacked.

607
00:37:23,341 --> 00:37:26,675
How about reading this
notation right here out loud?

608
00:37:27,011 --> 00:37:30,812
"No tetanus shot given. Patient
recently received booster."

609
00:37:31,249 --> 00:37:33,343
And how about
reading that notation?

610
00:37:35,754 --> 00:37:38,053
"Blood tests indicate
presence of narcotics.

611
00:37:38,223 --> 00:37:40,385
Patient confirms."

612
00:37:41,793 --> 00:37:43,352
You were taking narcotics?

613
00:37:44,162 --> 00:37:47,360
- Just some oxycodone, for my back.
- Oh.

614
00:37:48,299 --> 00:37:52,395
Ahem. Boy, you talk about luck.

615
00:37:52,971 --> 00:37:57,705
Here you are, all cut
up in some back alley,

616
00:37:57,876 --> 00:37:59,936
and you'd just taken
a tetanus booster

617
00:38:00,411 --> 00:38:03,779
and you're popping
industrial-strength painkillers.

618
00:38:04,082 --> 00:38:07,075
Couldn't have worked out
better if you'd planned it, could it?

619
00:38:07,252 --> 00:38:11,121
So, uh, when'd you take
that tetanus booster anyway?

620
00:38:12,791 --> 00:38:14,151
You know, I really
don't remember.

621
00:38:14,292 --> 00:38:16,818
Just rough. Was it a
year before the stabbing,

622
00:38:16,995 --> 00:38:20,397
or two months
before? A week before?

623
00:38:21,399 --> 00:38:25,564
Or was it the day before?

624
00:38:25,737 --> 00:38:27,296
Of course, it wasn't
the day before.

625
00:38:27,472 --> 00:38:29,304
You went to your
doctor the day before.

626
00:38:29,474 --> 00:38:32,410
I can call your secretary up
here. She's the one who told me.

627
00:38:32,577 --> 00:38:34,808
As I just said, my
back was hurting.

628
00:38:34,979 --> 00:38:36,777
Your back was fine!

629
00:38:36,948 --> 00:38:39,383
I saw you the day
before, remember?

630
00:38:40,118 --> 00:38:42,815
I went to the doctor
the day before the attack

631
00:38:42,987 --> 00:38:45,047
because my back hurt

632
00:38:45,223 --> 00:38:47,215
and he gave me a
prescription for oxycodone.

633
00:38:47,392 --> 00:38:50,260
- And a tetanus booster.
- No.

634
00:38:50,428 --> 00:38:52,294
I know how we can prove it.

635
00:38:52,463 --> 00:38:54,989
I'll get the notes the doctor
took during your appointment.

636
00:38:55,166 --> 00:38:58,102
Objection. Medical records
are privileged information.

637
00:38:58,269 --> 00:39:00,389
If you're telling the truth,
you have nothing to fear.

638
00:39:00,538 --> 00:39:03,303
- Mr. Matlock, you know better.
- But if the witness agrees,

639
00:39:03,474 --> 00:39:06,308
- they can be admitted, can't they?
- The witness is not on trial here.

640
00:39:06,477 --> 00:39:07,637
But it's germane to the case.

641
00:39:07,779 --> 00:39:09,839
I am not going to let
you make an issue of this.

642
00:39:10,014 --> 00:39:11,414
Objection sustained.

643
00:39:11,583 --> 00:39:13,814
Now, drop this line of
questioning and move on

644
00:39:13,985 --> 00:39:16,511
to another one,
Mr. Matlock. Now.

645
00:39:23,394 --> 00:39:27,490
As the owner of the
Drake Apartments,

646
00:39:27,665 --> 00:39:30,430
I imagine you have
a key to the building.

647
00:39:30,602 --> 00:39:32,264
The lock to the
front door is broken,

648
00:39:32,437 --> 00:39:34,702
- but you have a key?
- That's right.

649
00:39:34,873 --> 00:39:37,604
And I guess you have
keys to all of the apartments?

650
00:39:38,943 --> 00:39:40,206
I have a master key, yes.

651
00:39:40,378 --> 00:39:43,075
And that's how you broke
into Stan Johnson's apartment.

652
00:39:43,248 --> 00:39:44,408
I was never in his apartment.

653
00:39:44,549 --> 00:39:46,882
- And that's your blood got on his shirt.
- No.

654
00:39:47,051 --> 00:39:48,986
You knew he was
gonna take you to court,

655
00:39:49,153 --> 00:39:51,622
where George Mitchell,
the great philanthropist,

656
00:39:51,789 --> 00:39:54,953
would suddenly be exposed
as the greedy, deceitful

657
00:39:55,126 --> 00:39:56,458
slumlord that you really are.

658
00:39:56,828 --> 00:39:59,195
And you wanted that
least of all, didn't you?

659
00:39:59,364 --> 00:40:00,404
- Objection.
- Mr. Matlock.

660
00:40:00,565 --> 00:40:03,228
You came up with a plan to
make him look like the criminal

661
00:40:03,401 --> 00:40:04,494
and you the victim.

662
00:40:04,669 --> 00:40:07,138
You inflicted those
wounds on yourself.

663
00:40:07,305 --> 00:40:08,568
- Objection!
- That is a lie.

664
00:40:08,740 --> 00:40:09,833
You stabbed yourself

665
00:40:10,008 --> 00:40:11,806
- then you framed Stan Johnson.
- Objection!

666
00:40:11,976 --> 00:40:14,241
You're getting the sympathy
of the press and the court

667
00:40:14,412 --> 00:40:16,142
instead of the
contempt you deserve.

668
00:40:16,314 --> 00:40:17,338
- How dare you.
- Enough!

669
00:40:17,515 --> 00:40:19,882
- You know it's true, I know it's true.
- Objection!

670
00:40:20,051 --> 00:40:22,131
And if the court would
release those medical records,

671
00:40:22,287 --> 00:40:24,779
- we'd all know it's true.
- Mr. Matlock, you are out of order.

672
00:40:24,956 --> 00:40:26,788
I'm out of order?
He's out of order.

673
00:40:26,958 --> 00:40:30,156
My client could go to jail for
something this bum did to himself,

674
00:40:30,328 --> 00:40:31,819
and you say I'm out of order?

675
00:40:31,996 --> 00:40:35,831
What you are is out of control.
Guards, remove Mr. Matlock.

676
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:37,468
I'll tell you what's
out of order.

677
00:40:37,635 --> 00:40:40,662
Men like him commit
crimes and get away with it.

678
00:40:40,838 --> 00:40:42,033
That's what's out of order.

679
00:40:43,775 --> 00:40:46,939
It's that men like him can
make money off of poor people

680
00:40:47,111 --> 00:40:50,104
who have to live in these
filthy, rat-infested apartments.

681
00:40:50,515 --> 00:40:53,212
It's that men like him can
look at themselves in the mirror

682
00:40:53,384 --> 00:40:55,080
and not throw up.

683
00:40:55,253 --> 00:40:58,553
Rats running around
like houseguests.

684
00:40:58,823 --> 00:41:00,416
He took a painkiller and...

685
00:41:19,877 --> 00:41:22,244
The defense rests, Your Honor.

686
00:41:34,592 --> 00:41:36,083
Ben, this feels great.

687
00:41:36,260 --> 00:41:38,559
I thought for sure
I was going away.

688
00:41:38,730 --> 00:41:40,426
I never thought in
all the time I knew you

689
00:41:40,598 --> 00:41:42,032
I'd see you do what you did.

690
00:41:42,200 --> 00:41:44,396
Judge Clagett's
gonna have my head.

691
00:41:44,569 --> 00:41:46,060
Well, that's the bad news.

692
00:41:46,237 --> 00:41:48,001
The good news is, all
your ranting and raving

693
00:41:48,172 --> 00:41:49,663
gave the jury some
reasonable doubt.

694
00:41:49,841 --> 00:41:51,571
Boy, that sure was
something to see,

695
00:41:51,743 --> 00:41:53,939
and I wanna thank you
for that reasonable doubt.

696
00:41:54,112 --> 00:41:56,809
You said you wanted to do
something for me, you sure did.

697
00:41:56,981 --> 00:41:58,745
- Well...
- Only problem is

698
00:41:58,916 --> 00:42:00,756
George Mitchell got
out of there scot-free too.

699
00:42:00,918 --> 00:42:03,080
But he'll get his. We're
still gonna sue him, right?

700
00:42:03,254 --> 00:42:05,314
We're gonna sue him up one side

701
00:42:05,623 --> 00:42:08,024
- and down the other.
- Heh-heh-heh.

702
00:42:09,127 --> 00:42:10,493
Well...

703
00:42:11,462 --> 00:42:13,761
Time to see the
judge. You ready?

704
00:42:13,931 --> 00:42:16,958
Well, might as
well get it over with.

705
00:42:19,871 --> 00:42:23,205
Surprise!

706
00:42:38,423 --> 00:42:40,221
- Happy birthday.
- Thanks.

707
00:42:40,391 --> 00:42:42,053
- Friend?
- Friend.

708
00:42:42,226 --> 00:42:44,457
- Have some popcorn.
- Thanks.

709
00:42:44,629 --> 00:42:47,827
Let's go get the cake.
Wait till you see this cake.

710
00:42:53,704 --> 00:42:55,570
Judge Clagett.

711
00:42:56,474 --> 00:42:58,739
It's your birthday, Ben.

712
00:42:58,910 --> 00:43:02,369
We're old friends. I
hope we'll stay old friends.

713
00:43:03,614 --> 00:43:07,346
I know what you were doing
in here today, and it worked.

714
00:43:07,819 --> 00:43:11,415
But don't ever do that in my
courtroom again. Understood?

715
00:43:12,623 --> 00:43:15,525
- Yes, sir.
- Happy birthday, Ben.

716
00:43:17,128 --> 00:43:19,427
Here's the cake!

717
00:43:23,701 --> 00:43:24,725
Make a wish.

718
00:43:27,371 --> 00:43:28,930
Happy birthday.

719
00:43:38,749 --> 00:43:41,583
- Thanks a lot, Randy.
- You're welcome, Miss Stone.

720
00:43:47,225 --> 00:43:49,387
Hear you went
bonkers in here today.

721
00:43:49,827 --> 00:43:52,296
Yeah, I guess I did.

722
00:43:52,463 --> 00:43:55,729
- Had to haul you off and everything.
- Yeah.

723
00:43:56,734 --> 00:44:01,001
Well, Billy, I've been
in here a long time.

724
00:44:02,006 --> 00:44:04,032
Sometimes things happen.

725
00:44:04,208 --> 00:44:06,234
I don't know why.

726
00:44:06,511 --> 00:44:10,073
Sometimes I yell
when it calls for silence,

727
00:44:10,248 --> 00:44:13,013
and stand when
I'm supposed to sit.

728
00:44:13,518 --> 00:44:16,784
And sometimes I open my
mouth and things come out

729
00:44:16,954 --> 00:44:19,446
I didn't even
know were in there.

730
00:44:22,226 --> 00:44:25,321
- Yeah, it happens.
- Yeah.

731
00:44:25,496 --> 00:44:29,297
- Been in here a long time.
- Yeah.

732
00:44:31,369 --> 00:44:33,668
Okay, let's go. It's
been a long day.

733
00:44:33,838 --> 00:44:36,364
Yeah, but a great one.

734
00:44:43,281 --> 00:44:48,686
Uh, you all go ahead. I,
uh... I forgot something.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
